<GuidePage title='Blocking Requests - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 13 · Part B: Contain the Unknown</div>
  <h1>Blocking Requests</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Every await is a gate, and everything rendered behind it waits. The goal: make the region behind
    any single blocking request as small as possible, and make independent requests run at the same
    time, on the server and on the client alike.
  </p>

  <h2 id='scope'>13.1 What sits behind a request</h2>
  <p>
    When a component awaits, everything it renders waits with it. That's the
    <strong>blocking scope</strong> of the request, and it's determined entirely by
    <em>where in the tree the await happens</em>. An await at the top of the page puts the whole
    page behind one gate: header, navigation, static text, all of it held hostage by the slowest
    fetch. The same await, pushed down into the component that actually uses the data, holds only
    that region: the rest of the page renders and streams immediately (<a
      href='11-suspense-streaming.html#streaming'>§11.2</a
    >).
  </p>
  <p>
    So the rule is positional:
    <strong>await where the data is used, not where it's convenient to write.</strong>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> This is the same force behind
    <a href='14-minimize-the-wires.html'>§14</a>'s independence argument: self-fetching children
    don't just decouple the code, they shrink each request's blast radius to the component that
    needed it.
  </p>

