  <p>
    When to reach for a slot instead of a plain component: when the region deserves
    <strong>route-level independence</strong> - its own <code>loading.tsx</code>/<code
      >error.tsx</code
    >
    files, participation in navigation, or different content per sub-route. A canonical example: a page
    header. If the header is <em>static</em>, it's just layout markup - no slot needed. If the
    header <em>depends on server data</em> (program name, status badge, owner), a <code
      >@header</code
    > slot gives it an error and loading lifecycle that can't take the table down with it, and vice versa.
    One convention matters in practice:
    <strong><code>default.tsx</code></strong> is what a slot renders when the current URL doesn't match
    anything inside it (typically: re-render nothing-specific, or return
    <code>null</code>) - omit it and navigation into unmatched states 404s the whole route. Define
    it for every slot. <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>

