  <h3 id='slot-subroutes'>Nested routes inside a slot</h3>
  <p>
    Slots don't stop at one page. A slot folder can contain a <strong>full nested route tree</strong
    >
    of its own: give <code>@summary</code> an <code>archived/page.tsx</code> and the summary region now
    has two views, recent and archived, each with a file address.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> Because slots are not URL segments, the sub-route grafts onto the <em
      >page's</em
    >
    URL: navigating to <code>/programs/42/archived</code> means "render the slot subtree that matches
    <code>archived</code>", and the slot that matches is <code>@summary/archived</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interesting part is what happens to everybody else. On a <strong>soft navigation</strong> (a
    <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> click), Next.js performs a partial render: the slot whose subtree matches
    the new URL re-renders, and
    <strong>every other slot keeps its previously active state</strong>, even though the new URL no
    longer says anything about them. The header and the main content don't re-render, don't refetch,
    and don't flicker; one pane changes view. On a <strong>hard navigation</strong> (a reload, or a shared
    deep link), the router has only the URL to go on and cannot reconstruct what the unmatched slots were
    showing, so each of them renders its <code>default.tsx</code> instead (the implicit <code
      >children</code
    > slot included). This is exactly why the rule below insists every slot defines one: a missing <code
      >default.tsx</code
    > turns a reload of a perfectly valid URL into a 404.
  </p>

