  <h3 id='csr-depth'>When CSR runs deep: the tree serializes the network</h3>
  <p>
    The waterfall diagrams above show the network cost. There is a structural cost that appears the
    moment client-side rendering owns <em>layers</em> of content, and it's worse: the top layer cannot
    finish rendering until its API response lands, and a few layers beneath it sit components with data
    dependencies of their own, fetched with their own hooks, whose requests
    <strong>have not even started</strong>, because a component that isn't mounted can't fetch.
    Depth in the tree becomes serialization on the network, automatically, with nobody ever choosing
    it. Each layer resolves, renders, and thereby <em>begins</em> the next layer's wait: the user watches
    spinner generations hatch one inside another.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    TypeScript makes the trap explicit. When a child's props are typed non-optional (<code
      >stats: Stats</code
    >, not <code>Stats | undefined</code>), the child <em>literally cannot render</em> until the data
    exists, so the parent has exactly two options, and both are bad. <strong>Gate at the top</strong
    >: one <code>if (!data) return &lt;Skeleton /&gt;</code> and everything beneath waits, including children
    whose own fetches now serialize behind the gate. Or <strong
      >thread
      <code>undefined</code> down</strong
    > and let each element decide: now "can this render yet?" is smeared across every leaf in the region,
    ten scattered checks with ten fallbacks to size and maintain, instead of one. That entire decision
    space (gate or smear, and where, and with what fallback) exists <em
      >only because the data might not be there yet</em
    >. It is the concrete form of <a href='04-ssr.html#simplicity'>§4.2</a>'s argument: on the
    server the question is unaskable, because by the time anything renders, the data is a value.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-bad'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramSection.tsx - a gate at every depth</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramSection</span>({ programId }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data: program } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgram</span>(programId);

  <span class="tok-com">/* Nothing below exists until this resolves. The children's own
     fetches haven't started, because the children don't exist yet. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (!program) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SectionSkeleton</span> /&gt;;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;section&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">program</span>={program} /&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* Mounts only now; only now does its useProgramStats begin. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={program.id} /&gt;
    &lt;/section&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Every layer that repeats this shape adds a full round trip <em>plus</em> a render cycle to the path.
      Three such layers and the region's time-to-content is the sum of three sequential requests, none
      of which needed the others' data. The typed props made the gate feel mandatory; the gate made the
      waterfall.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramSection.tsx - when CSR is required: both questions asked at mount</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramSection</span>({ programId }: { programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Sibling hooks, no coupling: both requests start immediately,
     keyed by the id the component already has. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data: program } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgram</span>(programId);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data: stats } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgramStats</span>(programId);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;section&gt;
      {program ? &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">program</span>={program} /&gt; : &lt;<span class="tok-fn">HeaderSkeleton</span> /&gt;}
      {stats ? &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatRow</span> <span class="tok-prop">stats</span>={stats} /&gt; : &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}
    &lt;/section&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      One change de-serialized it: the stats hook is keyed by <code>programId</code> (already known) instead
      of <code>program.id</code> (born from the first response). And note what even the fixed version
      still pays: two render-or-fallback decisions instead of zero. That residual tax is permanent wherever
      CSR owns layered content, which is the deeper argument for putting the chain on the server in the
      first place.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

